Interview Question
Why do we use fork() before exec()?
Answer

fork() creates a child process so that the parent process (shell) remains alive.

exec() then replaces the child process with the desired program.

This allows the command to run while the shell continues to exist.

Another Example

Suppose terminal executes:

chrome

Without fork:

bash
  ↓
chrome

When Chrome closes:

shell gone

❌

With fork:

bash
   |
   +---- chrome

Chrome closes.

bash still alive

✅

Memory Trick
fork()
Create Worker
exec()
Give Worker New Job

So:

fork()
     ↓
Create Child

exec()
     ↓
Turn Child Into Desired Program
🎯 One-Line Interview Answer

fork() is used to create a child process, and exec() is used to load the required program into that child process, so the parent shell remains alive and can accept more commands after the program finishes.
